// scratch.golem
//
// Personal scratch file for exploring language design beyond what
// src/golemc.cpp currently supports. golemc, as of this writing, only
// supports one statement form (`print "string literal";`) - none of the
// declaration, expression, assignment, or typed-print forms below are
// implemented yet. This file exists to have a concrete target program to
// design and implement against, not as something that compiles today.
//
// Open question raised by the final line: does `print` implicitly format
// a non-string operand (here, a uint16) as decimal text, or does that
// require an explicit conversion? DECIDED (see
// docs/design/language-type-proposals.md, Axis 7 and Axis 8): `print` is
// monomorphic (string operands only); `str` is a real type and doubles as
// its own conversion function, so printing qux requires `print str(qux);`.
// Each `str(...)` call site gets its own compile-time-assigned buffer ID
// (populated on the fly, cleared when done) rather than a general heap.
// Still open: the hand-rolled divide-by-10 int-to-decimal routine itself
// isn't implemented yet, and `str`'s optional format argument
// (`str(qux, "fmt")`) is entirely TBD.

uint16 foo = 10;
uint16 bar = 5;
uint16 qux;
qux = foo + bar;
print str(qux);

// --- Speculative: what a Golem-source equivalent of
// examples/loop_test/loop_test.asm (the hand-assembled "print Hello 50
// times" tail-call-loop test - see
// docs/devlog/2026-07-29-loop-tail-call-prototype.md) might look like,
// using Axis 15's leaning-but-undecided `For ... To ... Next` counted-loop
// syntax. Not implemented - golemc has no loop/control-flow syntax yet.
//
// Deliberately counts UP (1 To 50) rather than down like the hand-written
// version's counter did, because Golem has no subtraction primitive
// designed yet (Axis 16 - `--`/binary `-` are blocked on a not-yet-designed
// two's-complement lowering). Counting up only needs `+`, which Axis 13
// already designed and golemc already implements - so this sidesteps that
// gap entirely rather than assuming it away.
//
// Left open deliberately: whether golemc would ever recognize that this
// loop's body compiles down to a self-referential conditional buffer call
// and therefore deliberately emit that call LAST in the loop's buffer so
// the VDP's own tail-call-to-jump optimisation applies (see the loop_test
// devlog) - or whether a first, naive codegen would just accept the VDP's
// ~20-deep call-stack limit as a real constraint on unrolled-loop depth
// for now. Also open: the exact runtime encoding of the loop counter `i`
// itself (a real Golem variable buffer vs. a compiler-internal one), and
// how/whether the doc's `VBUF_COND_F_BUFVALUE`-against-a-zero-buffer
// gotcha (see the same devlog) would need to surface as a codegen detail
// here, or could stay entirely hidden behind whatever comparison op `For`
// lowers to.
//
// `i` is declared on its own line rather than inline in the `For` header
// (i.e. NOT `For uint16 i = 1 To 50`) - Author's stated preference is to
// keep declaration and loop-iteration concerns separate, rather than
// `For` doubling as a declaration form. Open: whether golemc should then
// reject `For` over an UNdeclared variable (forcing this two-line shape
// always), or just allow inline declaration too as sugar on top of this.
//
// `For ...;` and `Next i;` both end in `;` - DECIDED on Axis 15: every
// *statement* ends in `;`, including block-delimiting keyword statements
// themselves, with zero exceptions. (Raised right here, drafting this
// very example - see Axis 15's updated "Relationship to Axis 5" note.)
// NB this is a statement rule, not a line rule - newlines remain
// insignificant (per Axis 5), so the multi-line layout below is purely a
// readability choice, not a grammar requirement. This whole loop would be
// equally valid crammed onto one physical line:
//   For i = 1 To 50; print "Hello "; Next i;
// - or even the entire rest of this file, for that matter. Statement vs.
// line is a real, deliberate distinction, not interchangeable wording.
uint16 i;
For i = 1 To 50;
    print "Hello ";
Next i;
